diff --git a/CHANGELOG.en.md b/CHANGELOG.en.md
index b4579ff..467aef0 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.en.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.en.md
@@ -10,11 +10,11 @@ Notable changes in Telsiz releases are listed in this file. Version numbers foll
 - Seven new themes: Field Radio (Saha Telsizi), Tube Radio (Lambalı Radyo), Phosphor (Fosfor), Polar Station (Kutup İstasyonu), Vinyl (Plak), Cockpit (Kokpit) and High Contrast (Yüksek Kontrast). Each has a dark and a light mode, and Arcade stays the default theme. The themes bring no new fonts, they use other combinations of the existing fonts. In Settings > Appearance the theme cards sit in the Radio Themes and Accessibility groups and are more compact: the preview strip is shorter and the description is visible only on the selected card (screen readers read the description of every card). High Contrast gives at least 7:1 contrast for text, uses thick borders, no gradients, shadows or background light, underlines links, draws a thick focus ring and sets disabled buttons apart with a dashed border instead of fading them. When the system has high contrast turned on (`prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors such as Windows High Contrast) and no theme preference is stored on the device, High Contrast is chosen on first start. This choice is not stored, and clicking the High Contrast card, which shows as selected in Settings, makes it permanent. In forced colors mode selected items get a border in the system highlight color. The total size of the stylesheets grew with the new themes.
 - Forgot my password (recovery code): a 28 character recovery code is shown when the account is created. The code can be copied or downloaded as a file, and the person continues after confirming "I saved the code". The code dialog does not close without this confirmation. If the page is reloaded or closed before it, the app warns on the next start and the status is highlighted in Settings > My Account. A person who forgets their password resets it themselves with the Forgot My Password link on the sign in screen using their username, recovery code and a new password: the security key does not change, old direct messages stay readable, the people they talk to see no key change warning, and a new code is issued once the code is used. The code is created, renewed or removed with the password in Settings > My Account > Recovery Code, which shows when and on which device it was created. When changing the password, the code can be renewed at the same time. Accounts without a code are reminded once after signing in with the password, and the "I don't have a recovery code" section on the sign in screen points these people to the frequency owner. A password reset by the owner or the command line also deletes the recovery code. The code is 16 random bytes, and the server stores only the scrypt hash of a key derived from the code and the private key wrapped with the code. The recovery endpoints give the same answer at the same cost when the account does not exist, has no code or the code is wrong, and their attempt counters are separate from sign in. There is no reset by email. The new endpoints `POST /api/me/recovery`, `POST /api/recover/keys` and `POST /api/recover` were added, an optional `recovery` field was added to the `POST /api/register` and `POST /api/me/password` requests and a `recovery` field with the creation time and device of the person's own code to the `GET /api/state` response, with the new error codes `bad_recovery` and `keys_changed`.
 - Moving between conversations: when a text room or direct conversation with unread messages opens, a New messages divider is placed in front of the first unread message and brought into the upper part of the visible area. The bar above the conversation shows the unread count and the time of the first unread message, Jump to Unread returns to the divider and Mark as Read reads the whole conversation. If the first unread message is not among the newest 50, the conversation opens around the last read message and newer messages arrive as you scroll down. The unread count now drops when you reach the bottom of the conversation, not when you enter the room. A message that arrives while you are scrolled up is not counted as read and does not scroll the page, and the pill at the bottom of the message stream says "1 new message below" and takes you to the bottom when pressed. A short notice that appears in the same place no longer blocks pressing the button under it. With no new message, the pill becomes Jump to Bottom when you are more than two screens up. The Stations list and sheet have a Direct messages group between the text rooms and the voice rooms: unread conversations first, at most six rows, and the rest open with All Direct Messages. Below 1280 pixels the Direct Messages button in the top bar opens the Stations sheet at this group, and on wide screens it opens the newest conversation with unread messages, or the last opened one. With no direct conversations this button opens the Friends view. The Room info sheet no longer has a direct message list, and in the direct message view the Room info button appears only when the sheet has incoming friend requests or Notifications to show. When the page reloads, an open direct conversation or the Friends view comes back too (before, only the last text room was remembered). As before, read marks are kept only on the device and are not synced between devices.
-- Moving between conversations 2: the last eight opened conversations are kept in memory (in encrypted form, decrypted text is not kept). Going back to a conversation draws the list without the skeleton and without asking the server, the first message in the visible area stays in place, and then the newest messages are fetched in the background and added. Messages that arrive, are edited or are deleted while you are in another conversation are applied to the conversation in memory too. On a resync, when the session starts or ends and when the key changes, the conversations in memory are not used. Older messages load without pressing the Load Older Messages button when you get near the top, the button stays and it works in older browsers too. Keyboard shortcuts: Alt+Up and Alt+Down open the previous and next conversation in the order of the Stations list, Alt+Shift+Up and Alt+Shift+Down open the next unread conversation (mentions and direct messages first), and Esc with no window open counts the conversation as read and jumps to the bottom. These shortcuts do not work when push to talk or another voice key is bound to Alt, Shift or the arrows. The shortcuts are also listed in the How to change frequencies guide (the ? button of the band). The Ctrl+K search now also shows text rooms and direct conversations whose name matches in a Rooms and People section (ignoring Turkish letters and letter case), and the chosen conversation opens.
+- Moving between conversations 2: the last eight opened conversations are kept in memory (in encrypted form, decrypted text is not kept). Going back to a conversation draws the list without the skeleton and without asking the server, the first message in the visible area stays in place, and then the newest messages are fetched in the background and added. Messages that arrive, are edited or are deleted while you are in another conversation are applied to the conversation in memory too. On a resync, when the session starts or ends and when the key changes, the conversations in memory are not used. Older messages load without pressing the Load Older Messages button when you get near the top, the button stays and it works in older browsers too. When a load fails, loading on its own stops for that conversation and the error is reported once, and the button retries. Keyboard shortcuts: Alt+Up and Alt+Down open the previous and next conversation in the order of the Stations list, Alt+Shift+Up and Alt+Shift+Down open the next unread conversation (mentions and direct messages first), and Esc with no window open counts the conversation as read and jumps to the bottom. These shortcuts do not work when push to talk or another voice key is bound to Alt, Shift or the arrows. The shortcuts are also listed in the How to change frequencies guide (the ? button of the band). The Ctrl+K search now also shows text rooms and direct conversations whose name matches in a Rooms and People section (ignoring Turkish letters and letter case), and the chosen conversation opens.
 
 ### Changes
 
-- At medium widths (760 to 1279 pixels) the Stations list shows in the right column above the radio card, so rooms and direct messages are reachable without pressing a button. The list is hidden on screens shorter than 720 pixels, on phones and when the radio card grows in a voice room and leaves no room for two rows of the list, and then the Stations button in the top bar shows. On phones the closed radio card no longer has the "Pick a voice room" bar that could not be pressed, and has a 44 pixel Voice Rooms button that opens the Stations sheet at the voice rooms instead. On touch screens room and sheet rows and the buttons of the top bar and the conversation header are at least 44 pixels high. On phones bottom sheets close when dragged down by their handle or header. The unread count at startup comes from the newest 50 messages, so 120 unread messages showed as "50". Now they show as "50+", and the count becomes exact once the conversation is opened.
+- At medium widths (760 to 1279 pixels) the Stations list shows in the right column above the radio card, so rooms and direct messages are reachable without pressing a button. The list is hidden on screens shorter than 720 pixels, on phones and when the radio card grows in a voice room and leaves no room for two rows of the list, and then the Stations button in the top bar shows. On phones the closed radio card no longer has the "Pick a voice room" bar that could not be pressed, and has a 44 pixel Voice Rooms button that opens the Stations sheet at the voice rooms instead. On touch screens room and sheet rows and the buttons of the top bar and the conversation header are at least 44 pixels high. On phones bottom sheets close when dragged down by their handle or header. The unread count at startup comes from the newest 50 messages, so 120 unread messages showed as "50". Now they show as "50+" (screen readers say "at least 50 unread"), and the count becomes exact once the conversation is opened.
 - The placeholder of the Add Friend field now reads "Enter a username" (before "username"). The field's label is only visible to screen readers, so the placeholder is the only visible hint.
 - The display name and @username on the profile card now sit to the right of the profile picture, with the role right next to the name. Before, the name, username and role were stacked below the picture. Plain members now also get a faint "Member" badge on their card, and someone with a custom role shows both badges. The My Account and Profile previews in Settings and the head of the avatar menu use the same layout and also show the custom role (the avatar menu used to write the role next to the username). If the name is long the role wraps to the next line, and if the card gets very narrow (for example on a phone with a large font size) the name moves below the picture. On a phone with a large font size the right edge of the card no longer runs off the screen.
 - In every theme text and user interface component colors are now measured on all nine surfaces, hover and selected backgrounds included (`scripts/kontrast.js`, `test/tema.test.js`). Colors that fell slightly below the threshold in this measurement were adjusted: the online and idle markers in Night Frequency light mode and the live color in Turquoise and Copper light mode became slightly darker, and the danger text and the input border in Turquoise and Copper dark mode became slightly lighter. Of the eight custom role colors shared by the light modes, seven became slightly darker and blue stayed the same. The colors of the Camera, Microphone, Deafen and Screen buttons on the radio card now come from tone tokens (`--tone-*`) that themes can override.
@@ -26,7 +26,7 @@ Notable changes in Telsiz releases are listed in this file. Version numbers foll
 - In the Arcade and Turquoise and Copper themes the border of the camera indicator on the radio card ("Your camera is on, everyone in the room can see it") and of the notice shown when someone starts sharing their screen was not drawn at all. The muted Microphone and active Camera buttons of a direct message call, the chip and panel of your own share, the Stop Watching button, the Telsiz DJ error box and the verified safety number button were also drawn in the text color instead of their own border color. Both themes now have live and danger border colors, and the borders show with at least 3:1 contrast on every surface.
 - Text and attachments in the message field stayed there when you moved to another conversation: text typed in a direct message and a photo attached to it remained in the message field after switching to a text room and could be sent to that room with the Send button. The same happened between text rooms. Every text room and direct message now has its own draft: when you leave a conversation its text and attachments stay with it and are back when you return, they never show up in another conversation's message field and cannot be sent there. An attachment that is still uploading finishes in its own conversation. Drafts are kept only in memory and never written to disk. They are deleted when you sign out and when you switch frequencies, the draft of a direct message is deleted when you block that person and the draft of a text room is deleted when the room is deleted. Because the server deletes uploads that are not attached to a message after an hour, an attachment left waiting in a draft for a long time could not be sent and every attempt showed "The files attached to the message are invalid." Such an attachment is now uploaded again from its file when you return to the conversation or press Send, the text stays in place and the message can be sent once the upload finishes. The upload queue handles the open conversation's attachments first, and an upload error in one conversation no longer marks the attachments of other conversations as failed.
 - The back button of the browser and the phone left the app after a few room and direct message switches. On phones it did the same while search, settings, a direct message or Friends was open, and at 768 and 1024 pixels wide while the Stations sheet was open. Room, direct message and Friends switches are now written to the browser history without changing the address. At every width the back button first closes an open sheet, search, settings, the image viewer or the profile card, then returns to the previous conversation, and the forward button works too. The app is left only when you press back from the conversation it opened with. Entering a room with unread messages jumped to the bottom and left the first unread message far above the screen, and a message that arrived while you were scrolled up was counted as read with no indicator (see the Moving between conversations item under New features).
-- In the direct message header the other person's name was not visible between 1024 and 1280 pixels (it shrank to a few pixels) and the Profile button overlapped the Room info button. Now when the header does not fit, Voice Call, Video Call, the safety badge and Profile become icons only and the name stays at least 6rem wide. Going back to a conversation downloaded the messages again every time, showed skeleton rows and jumped to the bottom. The Ctrl+K search could not find a room name and said "No results".
+- In the direct message header the other person's name was not visible between 1024 and 1280 pixels (it shrank to a few pixels) and the Profile button overlapped the Room info button. Now when the header does not fit, Voice Call, Video Call, the safety badge and Profile become icons only and the name stays at least 6rem wide (phones have no such limit, so the buttons fit). Going back to a conversation downloaded the messages again every time, showed skeleton rows and jumped to the bottom. The Ctrl+K search could not find a room name and said "No results".
 - The architecture document said a password could be reset by the owner, an admin or the command line. Only the owner can reset members' passwords, the document was corrected.
 
